{
  "module": "The transformer block",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "pre-vs-post-ln",
    "title": "Pre-LN versus post-LN",
    "summary": "The two places a LayerNorm can sit in a block, and why modern models use pre-LN: it keeps an untouched identity path, and its gradients behave at the start of training.",
    "covers": ["pre-vs-post-ln"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "preln-block",
        "reason": "One of the two layouts is the block just described."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "gradient-descent",
        "reason": "The difference is about how gradients behave in training."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "two-layouts",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A LayerNorm can sit in two places in a block. The "
            },
            {
              "text": "original Transformer",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " used "
            },
            {
              "text": "post-LN",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", and most modern models use "
            },
            {
              "text": "pre-LN",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "Post-LN: y = LN(x + Sublayer(x)). The norm comes "
              },
              {
                "text": "after",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "text": " the residual addition."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Pre-LN: y = x + Sublayer(LN(x)). The norm comes "
              },
              {
                "text": "before",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "text": " the sublayer, inside the branch."
              }
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 60, 62, 63)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "main-path",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The difference is in what lies on the "
            },
            {
              "text": "main path",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", the residual stream."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "In post-LN the normalisation is "
            },
            {
              "text": "on",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " the main path: every block's output passes through a norm. In pre-LN the main path is an "
            },
            {
              "text": "untouched identity path",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " with only sublayer outputs added to it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 63)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "training",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "It matters for training. Xiong et al. (2020) showed that with post-LN, at the start of training the gradients near the output are "
            },
            {
              "text": "large",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", so training needs a careful "
            },
            {
              "text": "learning-rate warmup",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " stage to stay stable."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Pre-LN gradients are "
            },
            {
              "text": "well behaved at initialisation",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". In their experiments, pre-LN Transformers trained "
            },
            {
              "text": "without",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " warmup reached results "
            },
            {
              "text": "comparable",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " to the baselines, with less training time and tuning."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The finding is about being easier to train, not about giving better results."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 63)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "in-practice",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "In practice, the pre-LN layout is used "
            },
            {
              "text": "from GPT-2 onward",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", with "
            },
            {
              "text": "one extra final norm",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " after the last block."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "That is the layout the block in the last lessons used."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 62)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Which formula is the pre-LN layout?"
            }
